@page "/"
@rendermode @(new InteractiveWebAssemblyRenderMode(prerender: false))

@using BgFolderAccess_Razor
@using BgQuiz_Blazor.Client.Quiz
@using XgFilter_Razor
@using XgFilter_Razor.Components
@using XgFilter_Lib.Filtering

@inject IFolderAccess FolderAccess
@inject PickedProblemFolder Folder
@inject AppliedFilter AppliedFilter
@inject FilterRestoreNotice FilterRestoreNotice
@inject MixVisibility MixVisibility
@inject MixDraft MixDraft
@inject ShuffleOption ShuffleOption
@inject QuizStatsStore StatsStore
@inject PickedFolderDocumentStorage DocumentStorage
@inject NavigationManager Nav
@inject QuizController Controller
@inject QuizLiveMarker Marker
@inject QuizSettings Settings
@inject QuizNoticeDismissal Notices

<PageTitle>BgQuiz</PageTitle>

@* app-busy: the progress-cursor idiom (app.css) whenever this page is working
   — a controller transition, a pick's scan-and-buffer, or an Apply's match
   count. The predicate is IsBusy for all of them, and the fieldset below
   reads the same one, so cursor and disabled controls cannot disagree. *@
<div class="container py-3 @(IsBusy ? "app-busy" : null)">
    <h1>BgQuiz</h1>

    @* Reload-reset notice: a full reload rebooted the runtime and discarded a
       live quiz. An outcome the user should understand, not an error — polite
       (matching the no-match notice), not assertive.

       An event notice, so dismissible (SPEC-notices.md §1), and the component
       holds the dismissal: the occurrence is this boot's reset, which lives in
       _showReloadNotice and dies with this page instance — the marker that
       raised it is cleared in the same step, so a navigate-back re-instantiates
       the page with nothing to show. No holder slot and no occurrence key: an
       owner that outlived the page would lengthen the notice's one-shot
       lifetime, and this instance never sees a second reset (SPEC-notices.md
       §2, the component-held case). *@
    @if (_showReloadNotice)
    {
        <Notice Kind="NoticeKind.Information" Announcement="NoticeAnnouncement.Polite"
                Dismissible="true">
            Your previous quiz was reset by the page reload — quiz progress
            doesn't survive a full reload.
        </Notice>
    }

    @* One disabled-while-busy boundary for the whole setup surface. A native
       <fieldset disabled> disables every form control inside — including the
       Apply buttons *inside* the imported FilterSurface/MixPanel, which expose
       no disabled parameter of their own — so no panel needs busy-awareness
       and no control can slip through unlisted. Bootstrap's reboot zeroes
       fieldset chrome, so layout is unchanged. *@
    <fieldset disabled="@IsBusy">

    <div class="mb-3" style="max-width:800px">
        <label class="form-label" for="pickProblemFolder">
            Problem-set folder
            <small class="text-muted">
                (a local folder of .xg / .xgp files — parsed in your browser, never uploaded)
            </small>
        </label>
        <div>
            <button type="button" id="pickProblemFolder" class="btn btn-outline-primary"
                    @onclick="PickFolderAsync">
                Choose folder…
            </button>
        </div>
        @* Supported-browser statement. Where the browser can serve neither pick
           mechanism the button is a dead entry point: it may raise nothing at
           all, and no code path here ever runs to explain it. Only a statement
           made *before* the gesture reaches that reader. Capability-shaped, not
           device-shaped — FolderPickDisplay records why the old phone framing
           was false in both directions.

           Deliberately NOT gated on _fsAccessAvailable, unlike the permission
           guidance below: the readers this is written for are exactly the ones
           that probe excludes. Gated only on "no folder held" — once a pick
           lands, the pick worked and the caution is moot — which is the same
           window its neighbour uses.

           Rendered from FolderPickDisplay, not a literal: Help's "before you
           start" lead states the identical rule, and a second surface is that
           class's stated bar for owning a phrase. Polite role="status", like
           every other outcome/advisory line on this page. *@
        @if (!Folder.HasFiles)
        {
            <div class="text-muted small mt-1" role="status">
                @FolderPickDisplay.SupportedBrowsers
            </div>
        }
        @* FS-Access pick guidance: the pick deliberately raises *two*
           browser-anchored prompts (folderAccess.js beginPick records why
           collapsing them to one is a closed won't-do), both easily missed, and
           neither says what declining costs — yet the two declines are wildly
           asymmetric. So name both grants and both consequences.

           Shown from page load, not only while the pick is in flight: knowing
           what is coming is only useful *before* the gesture that triggers it.
           So the note runs continuously from load, through the in-flight pick,
           until a folder is held — at which point its job is done and leaving it
           beside a populated summary would be stale noise. It reappears on
           Clear, and deliberately survives a *cancelled* pick: no folder is
           held, so the guidance is still the next thing the user needs.

           Still capability-gated (_fsAccessAvailable), not ungated: the fallback
           mechanism raises no permission prompt at all, so showing this there
           would promise prompts that never come — the same false-assertion class
           the wording below is written to avoid.

           Static, not stage-aware. Swapping the text as each prompt arrives was
           considered and DECLINED, not deferred: it needs a Blazor render to land
           between two back-to-back prompts on WASM's single thread, and the two
           arrive seconds apart, so one read covers both.

           Describes the grants; quotes no browser's prompt text — see
           FolderPickDisplay for why any such quote would be wrong somewhere.
           The lead-in promises no *count* of prompts on purpose: where the
           browser refuses to ask for the write grant at all (folderAccess.js
           catches that refusal and degrades — it is Chrome for Android's every
           pick), the second prompt never appears — so the list says what the
           browser may ask, not that it will ask twice. Step 2's consequence
           is worded "without it" rather than "decline and" for the same reason.
           Polite role="status". *@
        @if (_fsAccessAvailable && !Folder.HasFiles)
        {
            <div class="text-muted small mt-1" role="status">
                Your browser will ask about the selected folder, near the address bar:
                <ol class="mb-0 mt-1">
                    <li>
                        <strong>To view the selected folder's files — required.</strong>
                        Nothing works without it: decline and no folder is picked at all.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong>To save files into the folder — optional.</strong>
                        The quiz runs either way; without it,
                        @(FolderPickDisplay.WriteAccessConsequence).
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </div>
        }
        @* Silent-gesture account, the OTHER branch of the same probe the
           guidance above is gated on — one snapshot, two branches, each saying
           what its branch means. Where showDirectoryPicker is absent the hidden
           webkitdirectory input below is the only mechanism left, and whether a
           given browser HONORS it cannot be feature-detected: the attribute
           exists on the input object even where the picker never opens (observed
           on a tablet, halheinrich/backgammon#108, where Choose folder raised no
           chooser of any kind). So this gate does not claim the pick is dead —
           it claims the mechanism whose viability is undetectable is the one
           that will run, which is exactly what the probe can honestly report.

           The wording carries the rest. It is a CONDITIONAL, and asserts nothing
           about this browser: on desktop Firefox or Safari, where the fallback
           works, the antecedent is simply never met and no false claim has been
           made — the price is one grey line. A detection that overclaimed
           certainty would be worse than this hedge.

           Deliberately NOT gated on any pick state beyond "no folder held": the
           reader it is written for taps the button and sees nothing happen, so
           it must still be standing after that gesture. It is (the cancelled
           notice below appears beside it, not instead of it), and it goes away
           on the one event that proves the mechanism worked — a folder held.

           Names no vendor, no device class, no prompt count; quotes no browser
           UI. Polite role="status", like both advisories above it. *@
        @if (!_fsAccessAvailable && !Folder.HasFiles)
        {
            <div class="text-muted small mt-1" role="status">
                If choosing a folder opens nothing at all,
                @(FolderPickDisplay.DeadPickVerdict) — there's nothing more to
                try here.
            </div>
        }
        @* Fallback picker for browsers without showDirectoryPicker: a hidden
           webkitdirectory input, opened by the same button. Always rendered —
           not conditional on the capability probe — so the e2e suite can drive
           it directly. The probe now has two consumers with different jobs: the
           init-time snapshot above, which only decides whether guidance is worth
           showing, and the per-gesture probe in PickFolderAsync, which is the
           authoritative mechanism fork. This input is gated by neither. The
           FileList is read by the JS module (Blazor's InputFile can't see
           webkitRelativePath, which the top-level-only filter needs).

           Two landings, because this mechanism reports its two outcomes on two
           different events: change carries a selection, and cancel is the only
           signal a dismissal gives (no change event fires). The cancel binding
           is what keeps a dismissed fallback pick from ending on the screen the
           click just reset with nothing said — see _cancelledPickNotice for its
           best-effort nature. *@
        <input type="file" id="problemFolderFallback" webkitdirectory class="d-none"
               @ref="_fallbackInput" @onchange="HandleFallbackPickedAsync"
               @oncancel="HandleFallbackCancelled" />
        @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(Folder.Summary))
        {
            <div class="text-muted small mt-1 d-flex align-items-center gap-2">
                @* The caption is ProblemFolderLabel's, the one owner Home, Done
                   and Stats all render it through (halheinrich/backgammon#199);
                   Summary stays the holder's description of the pick and never
                   carries the caption. Home frames Summary because the file
                   count is what setup needs to see; the stats pages frame the
                   bare DisplayName. *@
                <ProblemFolderLabel Description="@Folder.Summary" />
                @* Clear and "Choose folder…" end the current setup identically —
                   one handler, so the two can't drift. *@
                <button type="button" class="btn btn-link btn-sm p-0 align-baseline"
                        @onclick="EndCurrentSetupAsync">
                    Clear
                </button>
            </div>
        }
        @* Truncated pick (issue halheinrich/backgammon#59): the folder held more files of some kind
           than that kind's cap admits, so a randomly drawn N were taken and
           the rest left unread. An outcome, not a failure — the quiz runs on
           what was read, and everything downstream (match count, mix, stats)
           derives from that partial pool — so polite like its neighbours, not
           the error.

           One line per truncated kind, and only for kinds actually truncated:
           the caps are per format and independent, so a folder can be far past
           one and nowhere near the other. Both figures come from the pick
           itself — the cap via PickTruncation.MaxFileCount, which reads the
           very constant the pick enforced, so the sentence cannot claim a
           limit the app does not apply.

           "chosen at random" is in the copy because the draw is OBSERVABLE
           (issue halheinrich/backgammon#106): re-picking one over-cap folder reads a different
           subset, so the match count and the problems shift between picks of
           the same folder. Unexplained, that reads as a bug; named, it reads
           as the reason to re-pick — the excess is reachable, not lost. The
           line still claims only how many were used and how many were not,
           never which: PickTruncation carries counts, no identities.

           Dismissible (issue halheinrich/backgammon#107), and the owner of the
           occurrence holds the dismissal (SPEC-notices.md §2): the app-scoped
           QuizNoticeDismissal, so navigating away and back cannot resurrect
           it, keyed on the pick's occurrence token so the next pick's report
           shows fresh with no reset wiring. *@
        @if (Folder.Truncations.Count > 0)
        {
            <Notice id="truncationNotice" class="mt-2" Kind="NoticeKind.Warning"
                    Announcement="NoticeAnnouncement.Polite" Dismissible="true"
                    @bind-Dismissed:get="Notices.IsDismissed(QuizNotice.PickTruncations, Folder.PickOccurrence)"
                    @bind-Dismissed:set="_ => Notices.Dismiss(QuizNotice.PickTruncations, Folder.PickOccurrence)">
                @foreach (var truncation in Folder.Truncations)
                {
                    <div>
                        Using @truncation.MaxFileCount @truncation.Extension files chosen at random;
                        @truncation.OmittedCount more @(truncation.OmittedCount == 1 ? "was" : "were") not read.
                    </div>
                }
            </Notice>
        }
        @* Pick-time stats status: which rung of the degrade ladder this folder
           landed on. Outcomes the user should understand, not errors — polite
           like the other outcome notices. Rendered only while a
           pick is held (the capability is meaningless otherwise).

           Dismissible (issue halheinrich/backgammon#107), held like the
           truncation notice above — one holder slot for all three branches,
           because they are mutually exclusive renderings of one per-pick
           verdict, so a dismissal can only ever cover the one the user actually
           read. Keyed on the pick's occurrence token: a re-pick reports its own
           verdict fresh.

           The Enabled branch's "will be saved" is a promise, so it is made only
           while neither stats forecast below says otherwise (issues
           halheinrich/backgammon#260, halheinrich/backgammon#261): with one in
           force, the forecast is the whole statement. StatsWillNotRecord reads
           the very store properties those notices render from — one fact
           decides both. *@
        @if (Folder.HasFiles)
        {
            @switch (Folder.Capability)
            {
                case FolderWriteCapability.Enabled when StatsWillNotRecord:
                    break;
                case FolderWriteCapability.Enabled:
                    <Notice id="statsCapabilityNotice" class="mt-2" Kind="NoticeKind.Information"
                            Announcement="NoticeAnnouncement.Polite" Dismissible="true"
                            @bind-Dismissed:get="Notices.IsDismissed(QuizNotice.PickStatsCapability, Folder.PickOccurrence)"
                            @bind-Dismissed:set="_ => Notices.Dismiss(QuizNotice.PickStatsCapability, Folder.PickOccurrence)">
                        Your lifetime quiz stats will be saved to
                        <strong>@QuizStatsFile.FileName</strong> in this folder.
                    </Notice>
                    break;
                case FolderWriteCapability.BrowserUnsupported:
                    <Notice id="statsCapabilityNotice" class="mt-2" Kind="NoticeKind.Warning"
                            Announcement="NoticeAnnouncement.Polite" Dismissible="true"
                            @bind-Dismissed:get="Notices.IsDismissed(QuizNotice.PickStatsCapability, Folder.PickOccurrence)"
                            @bind-Dismissed:set="_ => Notices.Dismiss(QuizNotice.PickStatsCapability, Folder.PickOccurrence)">
                        Your browser can't save quiz stats — the quiz runs without them.
                    </Notice>
                    break;
                case FolderWriteCapability.PermissionDenied:
                    @* Both halves come from FolderPickDisplay: the premise is
                       cause-agnostic (this rung is also reached by a browser
                       that refused to ask, with no prompt the user ever saw, so
                       "you declined" would attribute a decision they never
                       made), and the consequence is the same
                       clause the pre-pick guidance's step 2 promised — one
                       constant each, so warning and follow-through can't drift. *@
                    <Notice id="statsCapabilityNotice" class="mt-2" Kind="NoticeKind.Warning"
                            Announcement="NoticeAnnouncement.Polite" Dismissible="true"
                            @bind-Dismissed:get="Notices.IsDismissed(QuizNotice.PickStatsCapability, Folder.PickOccurrence)"
                            @bind-Dismissed:set="_ => Notices.Dismiss(QuizNotice.PickStatsCapability, Folder.PickOccurrence)">
                        @(FolderPickDisplay.WriteAccessNotGranted) — the quiz runs, but
                        @(FolderPickDisplay.WriteAccessConsequence).
                    </Notice>
                    break;
            }
        }
        @* The two will-not-record forecasts (issues halheinrich/backgammon#260,
           halheinrich/backgammon#261): what the pick-time probe already knows,
           said at the pick rather than after Start — the stats file exists and
           can't be read, or can't be written. Each is its own condition notice
           on its own holder slot, keyed on the pick's occurrence token like
           every notice in this band, and both may show at once. Polite warnings:
           outcomes the user should understand before acting, not failures.

           The copy is FolderPickDisplay's, which Help quotes. The Quiz and Done
           pages' Start-time and write-time notices stay regardless: the file
           can change between pick and Start, and a file another program holds
           open is only ever found at the write. *@
        @if (StatsStore.ForecastStatsUnreadable)
        {
            <Notice id="statsUnreadableForecastNotice" class="mt-2" Kind="NoticeKind.Warning"
                    Announcement="NoticeAnnouncement.Polite" Dismissible="true"
                    @bind-Dismissed:get="Notices.IsDismissed(QuizNotice.PickStatsUnreadableForecast, Folder.PickOccurrence)"
                    @bind-Dismissed:set="_ => Notices.Dismiss(QuizNotice.PickStatsUnreadableForecast, Folder.PickOccurrence)">
                @FolderPickDisplay.StatsUnreadableForecast
            </Notice>
        }
        @if (StatsStore.ForecastStatsUnwritable)
        {
            <Notice id="statsUnwritableForecastNotice" class="mt-2" Kind="NoticeKind.Warning"
                    Announcement="NoticeAnnouncement.Polite" Dismissible="true"
                    @bind-Dismissed:get="Notices.IsDismissed(QuizNotice.PickStatsUnwritableForecast, Folder.PickOccurrence)"
                    @bind-Dismissed:set="_ => Notices.Dismiss(QuizNotice.PickStatsUnwritableForecast, Folder.PickOccurrence)">
                @FolderPickDisplay.StatsUnwritableForecast
            </Notice>
        }
        @* Stats-retirement forecast (issue halheinrich/backgammon#146): this folder's stats file was
           written by an earlier version, so the next quiz will set it aside and
           start fresh. The act itself stays at the quiz bind — a pick must not
           mutate the folder (SPEC-stats-identity.md §3) — and the Quiz page's
           notice stays as its report; this is the same event in forecast tense,
           moved to where the consequence-bearing information belongs: before
           the action, like the "your data stays yours" line above the pick.

           Both tenses render the name from QuizStatsFile.RetiredNameFor over
           the version the file itself declared (surfaced by the pick-time
           probe), so the forecast and the report cannot name two different
           files, and a v1 holder is told about the v1 name.

           Reads below the capability notice, which it qualifies: that line says
           where stats will be saved, this one says what happens to what is
           already there. Polite — an outcome, not a failure — and dismissible,
           held on its own slot keyed on the pick's occurrence token, exactly
           like the two notices above it. *@
        @if (StatsStore.ForecastStatsSetAsideName is { } forecastSetAsideName)
        {
            <Notice id="statsRetirementForecastNotice" class="mt-2" Kind="NoticeKind.Information"
                    Announcement="NoticeAnnouncement.Polite" Dismissible="true"
                    @bind-Dismissed:get="Notices.IsDismissed(QuizNotice.PickStatsRetirementForecast, Folder.PickOccurrence)"
                    @bind-Dismissed:set="_ => Notices.Dismiss(QuizNotice.PickStatsRetirementForecast, Folder.PickOccurrence)">
                Your stats file was written by an earlier version of BgQuiz and can't be
                carried forward. When you start a quiz it will be set aside as
                <strong>@forecastSetAsideName</strong> — nothing is deleted — and a new
                <strong>@QuizStatsFile.FileName</strong> started, so your lifetime stats
                will begin again.
            </Notice>
        }
        @* Cancelled-pick outcome (not a failure): the pick ended holding no
           folder. Three causes reach here — the picker was dismissed, the
           required view-files permission was declined, or a present-but-inert
           showDirectoryPicker aborted without ever opening (issue halheinrich/backgammon#116,
           observed live) — and they are deliberately not distinguished
           (AbortError conflates them; pinned in BgFolderAccess_Razor), so the
           wording must be true under all of them and stay non-accusatory
           toward the user who simply backed out. Hence the advice is a
           CONDITIONAL ("if your browser asks…"): on the inert browser no
           request ever appears, and the old imperative "allow your browser's
           request" recommended an action that could not be taken. Silence
           (the earlier choice) left them on an unchanged, empty page with no
           explanation of why nothing happened. Polite, like its sibling
           outcome notices.

           The dead-chooser tail renders on the FS-Access branch only — the
           same one-snapshot two-branches discipline as the advisories above.
           On that branch the inert case lands here with nothing else on the
           page accounting for it, and the tail is the post-gesture sibling of
           the halheinrich/backgammon#105 conditional (same FolderPickDisplay verdict, its own
           antecedent), which is what breaks the retry loop the issue
           reported. On the fallback branch the tail is omitted because its
           antecedent is known false — this notice only fires there via the
           input's cancel event, which a chooser must open to raise — and the
           halheinrich/backgammon#105 grey line already stands beside this notice covering the
           silent case.

           Dismissible (issue halheinrich/backgammon#107), like the holder-backed
           notices above — but the page field is the notice's whole state, not
           the dismissal holder: the notice is per-visit by construction (it
           describes a gesture that left nothing behind), so its own transience
           already scopes the dismissal and an occurrence token would have
           nothing to outlive. Dismissing clears the field; the next cancelled
           pick sets it again and shows fresh. No @if around it — one fact,
           read once. *@
        <Notice id="cancelledPickNotice" class="mt-2" Kind="NoticeKind.Warning"
                Announcement="NoticeAnnouncement.Polite" Dismissible="true"
                @bind-Dismissed:get="!_cancelledPickNotice"
                @bind-Dismissed:set="_ => _cancelledPickNotice = false">
            No folder is picked, so there's nothing for BgQuiz to read yet —
            choose a folder and, if your browser asks to view its files,
            allow that request.
            @if (_fsAccessAvailable)
            {
                <text>
                    If no folder chooser opened,
                    @(FolderPickDisplay.DeadPickVerdict).
                </text>
            }
        </Notice>
        @* Empty-folder outcome (not a failure): a completed pick that yielded
           no problem files. Polite, like the no-match notice. Its page field
           is its whole state, like the cancelled notice above. *@
        <Notice id="emptyFolderNotice" class="mt-2" Kind="NoticeKind.Warning"
                Announcement="NoticeAnnouncement.Polite" Dismissible="true"
                @bind-Dismissed:get="!_emptyFolderNotice"
                @bind-Dismissed:set="_ => _emptyFolderNotice = false">
            No .xg / .xgp files found directly in that folder.
        </Notice>
        @* Pick failure: a gesture that failed, so assertive, and dismissible
           (SPEC-notices.md Fork A — an error already read should not sit until
           the next attempt). The message field is the box's whole state:
           dismissing clears it, and the next failure assigns it again and
           shows fresh even with identical text — the text was never the
           occurrence. No dismissed bit anywhere, and no @if around it. *@
        <Notice class="mt-2" Kind="NoticeKind.Error" Announcement="NoticeAnnouncement.Assertive"
                Dismissible="true"
                @bind-Dismissed:get="string.IsNullOrEmpty(_pickError)"
                @bind-Dismissed:set="_ => _pickError = null">
            <strong>Could not read the folder:</strong> @_pickError
        </Notice>
    </div>

    @* Progressive disclosure: the filters, saved filters, weighted mix, shuffle
       toggle, and Start button (with all their hints and notices) appear only
       once a folder with problem files is picked. Before a pick there is
       nothing to filter, weight, or start, so showing them is noise that buries
       the one required first step — picking a folder. Hiding them also makes
       "Apply the filters to enable Start" true by construction (the panel isn't
       there to apply pre-pick). Following the enclosing <fieldset>'s
       whole-setup-surface convention, this wrapper does not re-indent its body. *@
    @if (Folder.HasFiles)
    {

    @* The whole filter surface — saved filters, their degrade/refusal notices,
       and the filter panel — is one producer composite now: FilterSurface owns
       the panels and every interaction between them (load→stage, save/delete,
       the applied-state mediation onto the AppliedFilter holder, the notice
       copy), so this host binds facts and side effects only.

       Source is the pick's identity, minted once in CurrentFilterSource and
       read here and by every gate, so what a commit is keyed to and what a
       gate compares can never disagree; this region renders only while a
       folder is held, so a token always exists here.

       RestoreNotice is the app-scoped instance registered in Program.cs, bound
       and nothing more: its movers are producer-internal, so the composite
       decides entirely on its own when a boot's restored selection is
       announced and when the announcement dies. Storage is the picked-slot
       adapter — supplied only while the pick's
       capability exposes a readable directory handle, so a fallback pick
       (BrowserUnsupported) gets no saved-filters section at all, and
       PermissionDenied stays load-only through CanPersist with the host's
       FS-Access wording. The two events keep their panel-shaped contracts, so
       the match-summary side effects and gate re-derivation bind as before.

       Note the composite is UNMOUNTED whenever no folder is held (this gate),
       so its source-change rule cannot run for this host's pick/Clear gestures
       — EndCurrentSetupAsync documents the one line that covers the gap. What
       each successful pick gets instead is a fresh mount: Apply re-armed (the
       panel's committed config died with the old instance) and this folder's
       saved-filters document read through the seam. *@
    <FilterSurface AppliedFilter="AppliedFilter"
                   RestoreNotice="FilterRestoreNotice"
                   Source="CurrentFilterSource"
                   Storage="Storage"
                   CanPersist="Folder.Capability == FolderWriteCapability.Enabled"
                   PersistDisabledReason="@SavedFiltersDisabledReason"
                   OnFilterConfigChanged="HandleFilterConfigApplied"
                   OnAppliedStateChanged="HandleAppliedStateChanged" />

    @* Match summary on Apply: how many decisions the applied filters selected —
       the pre-mix pool the quiz (and any weighted mix) draws from — and what
       kinds of answer those decisions call for. Positions offering no play
       choice (a pass, or a forced play) are included because they auto-skip at
       quiz time, so the wording is "decisions that match", not "problems you'll
       see". Polite role="status"; advisory only
       (Start is gated by CanStart, not by this). The busy line shows during the
       first-parse after a pick.

       The count is filter-only by construction — SummarizeMatchesAsync passes
       QuizMix.Empty — so with a mix in effect the number describes the pool the
       mix draws *from*, which the quiz itself can fall far short of. Rather than
       leave that number to be read as the quiz's length, the caveat states the
       relationship; it rides inside the same role="status" region so the count
       and its qualification are announced together, and it derives from
       MixInEffect (checked ∧ non-passthrough, live per keystroke) so checking,
       unchecking, or clearing the mix updates it the moment it happens. Hedged
       ("can be"), not "will be smaller": a capless mix over Everything else can
       legitimately draw the whole pool. Pre-Start composition *preview* is a
       separate, deliberately unbuilt thing — a preview would have to compose
       against the lifetime stats, which is Start's work.

       The number is a count of distinct positions (the source stack dedupes by
       content identity), and saying nothing about that is what raised issue
       halheinrich/backgammon#104: a user reads their file count, reads this number, subtracts, and
       has no account for the difference except a bug. Two sentences close that.
       The first is standing — repeats are counted once, true of every pool — so
       the count is legible as a deduplicated count even where nothing
       collapsed. The second is the magnitude, and only the magnitude actually
       removes the mystery: "distinct" alone still leaves the subtraction
       unexplained. It renders only when something did collapse, because "left
       out 0" is a sentence about nothing.

       Both sentences claim exactly what the telemetry measures — matching
       decisions dropped as duplicates of a position already counted. Not files:
       a file can hold many decisions, and the magnitude is measured on the
       filtered stream, so any claim about how many *files* repeat would be a
       different number the app does not have. Neither sentence inventories what
       makes two positions the same; that is the producer's identity rule
       (SPEC-stats-identity.md §4) and restating it here would be a second
       spelling of it to keep in step.

       The standing sentence is suppressed on an empty pool for the reason the
       breakdown is: with nothing matched there is no pool to characterize.

       The breakdown answers a different question than the count does — not "how
       big is the pool" but "what is my collection made of", the curation-bias
       check a beta tester asked for. It is one number and five short pairs on
       the lines the count line already occupies, deliberately not a panel and
       not a disclosure: this page has just been through an information-hierarchy
       pass, and a click-to-open control here would either re-crowd it or hide
       the finding behind a gesture nobody knows to make. Buckets and labels come
       from AnswerTypeDisplay over the SAME AnswerTypeDistribution the count
       renders its Total from — one enumeration, one fold, so the parts cannot
       disagree with the whole. Zero buckets are rendered, not skipped: an
       answer type the collection has none of is precisely the signal.

       It rides inside the same role="status" region for the same reason the mix
       caveat does — a screen reader gets the pool and its make-up in one
       announcement rather than a number with a silent qualification.

       Named for its axis ("By answer type"), which leaves the region open for
       issue halheinrich/backgammon#3's pre-Start composition preview to sit beside it as its own
       block on the mix axis. Nothing here is built for that; it is only kept
       un-claimed. *@
    @if (_isCounting)
    {
        <div class="text-muted small mb-3" role="status">Counting matching decisions…</div>
    }
    else if (_matchSummary is { AnswerTypes: var pool, DuplicatesCollapsed: var collapsed })
    {
        <div class="text-muted small mb-3" role="status">
            <div>
                <strong>@pool.Total</strong>
                @(pool.Total == 1 ? "decision matches" : "decisions match") your filters.
                @if (pool.Total > 0)
                {
                    <text>Repeated positions are counted once.</text>
                }
                @if (collapsed > 0)
                {
                    <text>
                        That left out @collapsed more matching
                        @(collapsed == 1 ? "decision" : "decisions").
                    </text>
                }
                @if (MixInEffect)
                {
                    <text>
                        Your mix applies: the quiz is drawn from these matches rather than
                        presenting all of them, so the quiz itself can be much smaller.
                    </text>
                }
            </div>
            @* Suppressed on an empty pool only: with nothing matched there is no
               collection to characterize, and five zeros under "0 decisions
               match" would be noise where the page most needs to be quiet. That
               is not the zero-bucket case — a zero bucket inside a non-empty
               pool is a finding and always renders. *@
            @if (pool.Total > 0)
            {
                <div class="mt-1">
                    By answer type:
                    <ul class="list-inline d-inline mb-0">
                        @foreach (var bucket in AnswerTypeDisplay.Buckets(pool))
                        {
                            <li class="list-inline-item">@bucket.Label: <strong>@bucket.Count</strong></li>
                        }
                    </ul>
                </div>
            }
        </div>
    }

    @* Weighted mix — VISIBLE MEANS IN EFFECT (SPEC-filtering.md §5, ruled
       2026-09-07; halheinrich/backgammon#181). The one derivation,
       MixVisibility.IsVisible, is the user's standing setting AND this folder's
       stats fact. The second half asks what the folder holds — weighting
       composes FROM that record, so an empty one expresses no weighting anybody
       asked for, and missing, empty and unreadable are one answer, not three
       rungs.

       This condition is not a display rule with an effect rule beside it: it IS
       the effect rule. EffectiveMix reads the same member, so a visible mix
       always applies and a hidden one never does — the panel on screen is the
       whole disclosure, and there is no control here to arm.

       Nothing is disabled and no reason is given: the panel simply isn't here.
       The emergent behavior is accepted, not worked around — a brand-new folder
       offers no mix until its own first quiz has written some stats, whatever
       the setting says.

       Under a false derivation the mix plays no part in Start, by construction:
       with no panel mounted nothing re-hydrates, and EffectiveMix reads the
       passthrough, so no mix gate, warning, or refusal can fire. Keyed on the
       pick generation so every pick re-mounts it: the fresh mount triggers the
       discarded draft's re-hydration, which re-offers the stored last-valid mix
       — and, into a folder that has stats while the setting is on, re-offers it
       IN EFFECT. That is the ruling and not an oversight: the rows are a
       standing choice, the setting is a standing choice, and the panel showing
       them is how the user is told.

       The @key now decides less than it did: since the reset moved to the click,
       the enclosing HasFiles gate already unmounts this panel on every pick. The
       key stays because it — not the gate — is what guarantees the remount, and
       nothing here should depend on the gate's timing. *@
    @if (MixVisibility.IsVisible)
    {
        <MixPanel @key="Folder.PickGeneration" />
    }

    @* Disabled — not rewritten — while a committed mix owns presentation
       order: ShuffleOption keeps the user's value, so clearing the mix
       restores their prior shuffle preference untouched. *@
    <div class="mb-3 form-check">
        <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox" id="shuffleOrder"
               checked="@ShuffleOption.Enabled" disabled="@MixOwnsOrder"
               @onchange="HandleShuffleToggled" />
        <label class="form-check-label" for="shuffleOrder">
            Shuffle order
            <small class="text-muted">
                @(MixOwnsOrder
                    ? "(order comes from the mix's Random order setting)"
                    : "(present problems in random order instead of file order)")
            </small>
        </label>
    </div>

    @* The busy half is redundant under the enclosing fieldset, but explicit:
       the gate condition and the busy condition are both visible here. *@
    <button class="btn btn-primary"
            disabled="@(!CanStart || Controller.IsBusy)"
            @onclick="StartQuizAsync">
        Start Quiz
    </button>

    @* Four sibling hints, one per Start gate, in the gate's own order: each
       states its reason, and exactly one shows at a time (the chain mirrors
       CanStart's conjunction, most-fundamental first). The zero-pool hint
       renders only on a KNOWN-empty pool — a resolved summary with Total 0 —
       never while the count is null or still running, matching the gate. The
       mix hint is the ruled fix-or-turn-off sentence: the panel stays visible
       (the setting records the choice), Start goes dark, and the panel's own
       validation line says what specifically is broken. *@
    @if (FilterInEffect is null)
    {
        <small class="text-muted ms-2">Apply the filters above to enable Start.</small>
    }
    else if (!Folder.HasFiles)
    {
        <small class="text-muted ms-2">Pick a folder containing .xg / .xgp files to enable Start.</small>
    }
    else if (_matchSummary is { AnswerTypes.Total: 0 })
    {
        <small class="text-muted ms-2">No problems match the filters — adjust and re-apply them to enable Start.</small>
    }
    else if (EffectiveMix is null)
    {
        <small class="text-muted ms-2">Mix applies but isn't valid — fix it or turn the mix off.</small>
    }

    @* Start failure: assertive and dismissible, with the message field as the
       box's whole state — the pick error's shape, for the pick error's
       reasons. *@
    <Notice class="mt-3" Kind="NoticeKind.Error" Announcement="NoticeAnnouncement.Assertive"
            Dismissible="true"
            @bind-Dismissed:get="string.IsNullOrEmpty(_startError)"
            @bind-Dismissed:set="_ => _startError = null">
        <strong>Could not start quiz:</strong> @_startError
    </Notice>

    @* Weighted-start refusal: the effective mix has entries but no lifetime
       stats are available, and running silently unweighted would substitute
       content the user didn't ask for. Actionable and assertive — the
       one-click per-run escape sits inside the notice; the mix rows and the
       checkbox are never touched by either path (the escape is per-run, and
       unchecking is the user's own standing off-switch). The copy no longer
       points at Clear mix: under screen-follows-storage Clear genuinely
       deletes the rows, so the honest keep-your-mix escape is uncheck.

       A gate reason, so not dismissible (SPEC-notices.md Fork C): closing it
       would take the escape button with it and leave a Start click that did
       nothing, with no account of why. *@
    @if (_mixRefused)
    {
        <Notice class="mt-3" Kind="NoticeKind.Warning" Announcement="NoticeAnnouncement.Assertive">
            <p class="mb-2">
                Lifetime stats aren't available for this quiz
                (@MixDisplay.RefusalReason(StatsStore.Status)),
                so the weighted mix can't be applied.
            </p>
            <button class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm" id="startWithoutMix"
                    @onclick="StartWithoutMixAsync">
                Start without mix
            </button>
            <span class="small text-muted ms-2">
                runs this one quiz unweighted — or turn the mix off in
                <em>Settings</em> to stop weighting altogether; your mix rows are
                kept either way.
            </span>
        </Notice>
    }

    @* Empty-result outcome (not a failure): a valid Start that admitted no
       showable problem. Polite, not the error banner's assertive
       announcement. A gate reason — it is why Start found nothing — so not
       dismissible (SPEC-notices.md Fork C). *@
    @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(_noMatchNotice))
    {
        <Notice class="mt-3" Kind="NoticeKind.Warning" Announcement="NoticeAnnouncement.Polite">
            @_noMatchNotice
        </Notice>
    }
    } @* end progressive-disclosure @if (Folder.HasFiles) *@

    </fieldset>

    @* The way back to a running quiz (issue halheinrich/backgammon#58). Home is the third page
       reachable mid-quiz with no way back — Help and Settings already carried
       this affordance, and this is the same predicate and the same words, so a
       user who has met it once meets the same one here.

       Not the shared ReturnControl those pages render now
       (halheinrich/backgammon#241), deliberately: that control falls back to
       "Back to Home" with no quiz live, and Home is the setup page — it does
       not return to itself. So here the button is conditional, and absent
       when there is no quiz to go back to.

       Mid-quiz visits to Home are already safe by design — the picked files are
       read at Start only, and the setup surface's own gestures (Clear, re-pick)
       are pinned not to disturb a running quiz — so this adds a way back and
       nothing else. No guard, no warning.

       OUTSIDE the busy fieldset, deliberately: this navigates and drives no
       transition, so it follows the Show-stats / Back-to-setup convention of
       staying live while the page works. Inside, the fieldset's disabled would
       take the way back away exactly while a long parse makes a user most likely
       to want it. *@
    @if (Controller.HasStarted && !Controller.IsFinished)
    {
        <button class="btn btn-primary mt-3" @onclick="BackToQuiz">
            Back to quiz
        </button>
    }

    @* Version + beta feedback, one footer line. The link sits here because the
       version is what makes a report actionable and this is where the version
       already is — a tester reads the build they are on and mails about it in
       one gesture, with no chance of quoting the wrong one: both halves come
       from AppInfo, so the subject can't drift from the footer.

       Explicit-expression parens: bare "v@AppInfo.Version" is parsed as an
       email literal. *@
    <div class="text-muted small mt-4">
        <span id="appVersion">v@(AppInfo.Version)</span>
        · <a href="@AppInfo.FeedbackMailto">Send feedback</a>
    </div>
</div>
